\pard \fs20\tx-1440\tx-468\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\tx9360\tx10080\tx10800\tx11520\tx12240\tx12960\tx13680\tx14400\tx15120\tx15840\tx16560\tx17280\tx18000\tx18720
{\plain \fs36 \i\f1\fs36 Concerning the Psalter}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 The Psalter is a body of liturgical poetry. It is designed for vocal,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 congregational use, whether by singing or reading. There are several\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 traditional methods of psalmody. The exclusive use of a single method\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 makes the recitation of the Psalter needlessly monotonous. The\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 traditional methods, each of which can be elaborate or simple, are the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 following:\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \b\f1\fs20 Direct recitation}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  denotes the reading or chanting of a whole psalm, or\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 portion of a psalm, in unison. It is particularly appropriate for the psalm\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 verses suggested in the lectionary for use between the Lessons at the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Eucharist, when the verses are recited rather than sung, and may often be\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 found a satisfactory method of chanting them.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \b\f1\fs20 Antiphonal recitation}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  is the verse-by-verse alternation between groups of\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 singers or readers; e.g., between choir and congregation, or between one\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 side of the congregation and the other. The alternate recitation concludes\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 either with the Gloria Patri, or with a refrain (called the antiphon) recited\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 in unison. This is probably the most satisfying method for reciting the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 psalms in the Daily Office.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \b\f1\fs20 Responsorial recitation}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  is the name given to a method of psalmody in\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 which the verses of a psalm are sung by a solo voice, with the choir and\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 congregation singing a refrain after each verse or group of verses. This\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 was the traditional method of singing the Venite, and the restoration of\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Invitatory Antiphons for the Venite makes possible a recovery of this\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 form of sacred song in the Daily Office. It was also a traditional manner\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 of chanting the psalms between the Lessons at the Eucharist, and it is\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 increasingly favored by modern composers.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \b\f1\fs20 Responsive recitation}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  is the method which has been most frequently used\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 in Episcopal churches, the minister alternating with the congregation,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 verse by verse.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}\column 
\pard \fs20\tx-1440\tx-468\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\tx9360\tx10080\tx10800\tx11520\tx12240\tx12960\tx13680\tx14400\tx15120\tx15840\tx16560\tx17280\tx18000\tx18720
{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 The version of the Psalms which follows is set out in lines of poetry. The\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 lines correspond to Hebrew versification, which is not based on meter or\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 rhyme, but on parallelism of clauses, a symmetry of form and sense. The\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 parallelism can take the form of similarity (The waters have lifted up, O\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Lord / the waters have lifted up their voice; / the waters have lifted up\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 their pounding waves. }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Psalm 93:4}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 ), or of contrast (The Lord knows the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 ways of the righteous; / but the way of the wicked is doomed. }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Psalm 1:6}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 ),\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 or of logical expansion (Our eyes look to the Lord our God, / until he\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 show us his mercy. }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Psalm 123:3}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 ).\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 The most common verse is a couplet, but triplets are very frequent, and\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 quatrains are not unknown; although quatrains are usually distributed\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 over two verses.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 An asterisk divides each verse into two parts for reading or chanting. In\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 reading, a distinct pause should be made at the asterisk.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Three terms are used in the Psalms with reference to God: }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Elohim}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 ({\u8220\'93}God{\u8221\'94}), }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Adonai}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  ({\u8220\'93}Lord{\u8221\'94}) and the personal name }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 YHWH}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 . The {\u8220\'93}Four-\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 letter Name{\u8221\'94} (Tetragrammaton) is probably to be vocalized Yahweh; but\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 this is by no means certain, because from very ancient times it has been\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 considered too sacred to be pronounced; and, whenever it occurred,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Adonai}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  was substituted for it. In the oldest manuscripts, the Divine\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Name was written in antique and obsolete letters; in more recent\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 manuscripts and in printed Bibles, after the invention of vowel points, the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Name was provided with the vowels of the word }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Adonai}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 . This produced\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 a hybrid form which has been transliterated {\u8220\'93}Jehovah.{\u8221\'94}\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 The Hebrew reverence and reticence with regard to the Name of God has\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 been carried over into the classical English versions, the Prayer Book\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Psalter and the King James Old Testament, where it is regularly rendered\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 {\u8220\'93}Lord{\u8221\'94}. In order to distinguish it, however, from {\u8220\'93}Lord{\u8221\'94} as a translation\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 of }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Adonai, YHWH}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  is represented in capital and small capital letters:\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 L}{\plain \fs20 \scaps\f1\fs20 ord}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 .\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 From time to time, the Hebrew text has }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Adonai}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  and }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 YHWH}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  in\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 conjunction. Then, the Hebrew custom is to substitute}{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20  Elohim}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  for\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 YHWH}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 , and our English tradition follows suit, rendering the combined\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 title as {\u8220\'93}Lord G}{\plain \fs20 \scaps\f1\fs20 od}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 .{\u8221\'94}\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}\column 
\pard \fs20\tx-1440\tx-468\tx0\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\tx9360\tx10080\tx10800\tx11520\tx12240\tx12960\tx13680\tx14400\tx15120\tx15840\tx16560\tx17280\tx18000\tx18720
{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 In two passages (}{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Psalm 68:4}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20  and }{\plain \fs20 \i\f1\fs20 Psalm 83:18}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 ), the context requires that\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 the Divine Name be spelled out, and it appears as Y}{\plain \fs20 \scaps\f1\fs20 ahweh}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 . A similar\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 construction occurs in the Canticle, {\u8220\'93}The Song of Moses.{\u8221\'94}\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 The ancient praise-shout, {\u8220\'93}Hallelujah,{\u8221\'94} has been restored, in place of its\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 English equivalent, {\u8220\'93}Praise the Lord.{\u8221\'94} The Hebrew form has been used,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 rather than the Latin form {\u8220\'93}Alleluia,{\u8221\'94} as being more appropriate to this\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 context; but also to regain for our liturgy a form of the word that is\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 familiar from its use in many well-known anthems. The word may, if\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 desired, be omitted during the season of Lent.}{\plain \fs44 \f1\fs44 \par
}\column 
